A||N||16||Bigfieroman||Turned left, then the oil "left?"||||1||
Z||000000||Bigfieroman||06-11-2001||01:33 PM||||I was in a local parking lot, turning around (left) (180 deg, full lock) at maybe 10-15 mph, and the oil pressure dropped to nothing!  I thought the gauge just broke or something, and then it started tapping, losing power, and sounding really really bad.  I stopped after about 1 minute.  Anyone know what happened?  5w-30, 87 gt' auto, 101k.  I had it towed to the local shop, and I would like to tell them what to look for to save labor.  Thanks all.<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||06-11-2001||02:52 PM||||If you spun the car around and lost oil pressure for a few seconds then nothing should of happened because of the oil.<P>  same thing happens when you change your oil and filter, you have no oil pressure for a few seconds when you first start the car up.<P>  But if you left out one minor detail in your account, if you were turning the car around, 180°, at 15 mph, and oh by the way, the engine was rev'ing 6000 rpms at the time and the tires were smokin<P>  and your oil pump sucked air for 'about a minute' at 6000 rpm<P>  then: main bearings, rod bearings...<P>  if thats 'the rest of the story' then its rebuild time.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-11-2001||02:57 PM||||On the other hand, if you wernt reving the begezus out of it, and its only a coincedience that it happend when you were turning, then it could be anything, broken ring, stuck valve, timing slipped.<P>   can you defind 'Sounded really bad' a little more specifically?||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||06-11-2001||04:14 PM||||A lot of possibilities here.  To save labor have them go right into the pan.  I would suspect the oil pickup picked up something bad (clog) or came off somehow.  You also could have broken the oil pump shaft or oil pump components.  On other thing is the pressure relief valve on the oil pump.  All of these are inside the pan/block.  This all would be coincidental with the left turn of course.  I (along with many others) have made left turns frequently and some of them somewhat spirited so I don't think the turn was the root cause.  One minute is probably too long to run it with low blood pressure so have them check bearings & replace the pump & shaft while it is apart.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||06-11-2001||04:18 PM||||Go for the simplest explanation first.  You're a quart low on oil.  My '88 Formula oil pressure will drop in hard left hand corners when I'm low on oil.  It doesn't do it in right hand corners. Remember, transverse engine - oil pump driven by distributer.  When you take a hard left, you throwing all the oil away from the oil pickup, so if you're low, you're gonna suck air and loose pressure.  Shouldn't damage the engine, IF, it's only for an instant.  Still isn't good for it though.  Check and top off the oil, then try it again.  I think you'll see a difference.<P>Cornering also tells me when it's time to get gas!  Hard cornering throws the gas away from the pickup, the car sputters for a bit, then I drive sedately to the next gas station.  If I don't slosh the gas around, I have plenty to get there!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Who cares if the gas gauge isn't accurate?  I prefer the g<B>ass</B> gauge!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000005||Bigfieroman||06-11-2001||04:37 PM||||No Ken, I was not smoking the tires...as I said, automatic.  The engine started with a slight ticking sound, then it got louder, and then it just sounded like everything was rubbing togather, a whole cacaphony of tapping, ticking, etc.  At most it was 2000 rpm, and I was driving normally for that "about a minute" not really turning.  The turn itself was about 5-6 seconds at most.  It has done this before, going around this one hard left near my friend's house, where the pressure goes from 40 psi to 0, and as soon as I come out of the turn and hit the gas everything returns to normal.  This time it just didn't.  Ken, the way you replied at first was kind of ignorant and assumative, and I didn't really apprecciate that.  I may be 17, but I am not about to do donoughts with my brand new tires.  I have never even done one.  The only time I drive fast, or as the typical 16-17 year old does, is in our local park, where there are little used twisty roads with alot of elevation changes and several sweepers etc.  If I was doing a doughnut, I would have said so.  You folks aren't my parents or the police from this area, so I don't really care what you know about me, because with you it cannot come back to bite me in the ass.  Thank you for your other, nonsuggestive comments though.  That goes for the rest of you all too, thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cscougar||06-11-2001||04:52 PM||||There is a windage tray that you can put on the oil pan to help with oil problems at high speed turns.  Keeps the oil in the center of the pan and not sloshing to the side.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||06-11-2001||04:58 PM||||I had an 88 6000 with a 2.8 that the oil light would come on only in a left hand turn. Turned out the pickup tube had come out of the oil pump. The pump sits deep enough in the pan that it got oil the rest of the time. The Fiero 2.8 is the same basic engine design, so that's a possibility for your problem before.<P>Now it sounds like you have a new problem. Assuming you are on the full mark on oil, it sounds like your oil pump may have died (possibly from running dry???). Could also be bearings as mentioned above. Sounds like they will have to drop the oil pan and see what they see. Unfortunately, it sounds like lots of $$$ for a shop.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Good luck.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DJRice||06-11-2001||07:20 PM||||If you ran it for a minute or two with no oil pressure, then the sounds you heard are the sounds of money flowing very quickly out of your wallet as you rebuild your engine.<P>--<BR>Dillon||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfiero||06-11-2001||07:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>Go for the simplest explanation first.  You're a quart low on oil.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This reminds me of my newest GT.  I bought it in Oklahoma and drove it home to Denver.  At the first gas stop I attempted to check the oil......unfortunately I couldn't.  The dipstick tube was broken in half and I couldn't find the other half (engine was too hot to feel for it).  I figured it wouldn't matter.....it couldn't be that low.  <P>I made it home and drove it for the rest of the week before I got around to changing the oil.  The oil pressure would drop to zero whenever I went around a corner or accelerated hard.  I assumed the gauge was faulty, but when I changed the oil I discovered that I was a little low on oil.  I only drained 1 quart!!  I was about 4 quarts low!  Ouch.  Luckily the engine survived (it actually runs pretty good).<P>It is still possible that the oil pickup tube came off, but I won't know until after I pull the engine out (will be installing a fresh 3.2 soon).<P>Just thought I'd share.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000010||BAM-BAM||06-11-2001||07:59 PM||||  YO, BFM, slow down brutha, I don't think that anyone is trying to assume anything based on your age (which I don't think they new untill you told them). All that was asked was to try and be a little more informative about the circumstances. Let me tell ya brutha, age has no place in the art of spinning doughnuts. "Kids" of all ages like to stretch the legs every once in a while. But do this, it might save you a bunch of dough. Tell the shop to drop the pan and see what's up under there. If it's bad, you should opt for 1. a home rebuild with some bore work (3.1-3.2) or 2. replace the broke ass 2.8 with a like engine, which can be found rather easily and not to expensive. Or 3. go for the swap. A 3.4 is economical and plenty of go fo the dough. What ever you decide, do what you can yourself. Even if it means getting dad or a friend, or a friends dad to help you. A shop will outright "RAPE" your young butt. Besides, there's no better way to become intimately familiar with your ride than to bring her back to life  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Just my 2!!<P>Peace, B2 OUT! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||09:38 AM||||Yeah geeze big guy<P>I know you are feeling really bad over the sounds coming from your engine compartment, and the lack of power from the same place<P>and I always hate to hear that anyones engine is leaking all its noise out at once (noise is what makes a motor work, BTW, and if it all leaks out at once the motor is spent)<P>go back and look at your original post. in essensce it states 1 + 1 = $2,500.00. something is missing there.<P>and im pretty sure i have no kids running around that I dont know about, so you're right, I aint your papa<P>and I dont see you age posted anywhere.<P>after reading your other post, it makes a little more sense now- you turned the car around 180° with the steering hard over at 10 - 15 mph (pretty quick)<P>the oil light came on<P>the oil light STAYED on (you didnt mention this - i assumed it went back off)<P>you kept driving normally (2000 RPM, but you were in a parking lot, so I dont know what that means - 15mph?! my V6 auto does about 50 at 2000 RPM)<P>the oil light STAYS on, and after a minute you stopped.<P> Its not clear where the tapping and screehing metal noises in the engine started - during the turn, after 30 seconds, progressively got worse for the whole minute.<P>  The general rule for the oil light or oil pressure gauge is, if you lose oil pressure for more than a second, put the car in neutral and shut the motor down. the vacuum reserve in your brake system will have enough for a reasonable stop, you dont have power steering, so no matter how fast you are going you should be able to drop it into neutral, stop the motor, and coast/brake to a stop on the shoulder of the road, no matter where you are.<P>  Literally no matter where you are, in the middle of a tunnel, on a long bridge, dont let the motor spin for more than a sec with no oil pressure. Let the A-holes behind you all lean on their horns together - you dont have to grind your motor into a pulp so they can get where they are going 60 seconds sooner.<P>  then you get out, open the front hood and get the bottle of oil you always carry around wrapped up in an old Tshirt by the spare tire, and you put it in the motor. Dont need to check the oil level, just put it in. This should take less than a minute.<P>  then you start the car. If the oil pressure doesnt come back up in 3 or 4 seconds at idle, you shut it back down and call the tow truck.<P>   I assumed everyone already knew this, and that nobody would drive with no oil pressure for more than a second or two.<P>  There is nothing wrong with spinning your wheels if you feel like it, if you understand you are taking about $5 worth the tire off everytime you do. In fact its important to know all the aspects of how your car handles and performs - If you try to pull out into traffic too quick, and your wheels spin and you only make it half way out infront of a semi - its important to know what your car does under all conditions, including throttle WFO.<P>  and BTW a V6 auto will spin its wheels nicely, esp with the steering wheel hard over.<P>   I think, regardless of how or why it happened, from the description of the noises you heard from your engine compartment, it sounds like your motor is toast. I think that is why nobody wanted to answer your post - no one likes to breaks the bad news.<P>  I know exactly how it feels to be 17 and summer is here, and your car is sitting in the driveway with a big oil spot under it and the motor is blown. So I wont hold it against you if you are totally PO'd.<P>  Keep in mind, you never know who might be reading this forum, in the next town over, or county, or state, and might be in a position to help you out, with a used block, motor, donor car, whatever.<P> Im not trying to play games with your head - I dont have a spare motor you can put in your machine - but it would be a crime, with all the fiero owners on this forum, if someone cant help a 17 yr old get his car running again with some good advice, and maybe some good deals on parts, a short block or used motor. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Bigfieroman||06-12-2001||12:14 PM||||Sorry Ken, I've misjudged you. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||kslish||06-12-2001||02:53 PM||||Bigfieroman,<P>Any diagnosis on the engine yet?  If it turns out to be real bad, maybe the West Penn Fiero Club can help.  We have a lot of knowledgeable members and most do their own work and can usually recommend local mechanics in the area (ones that won't screw you over) to do the real hard stuff.  <P>We have a meeting coming up on the 19th at 7:00pm.  E-mail me for directions if you're interested, we're meeting at Larry's RoadHouse on Rt. 51 which isn't that far of a drive from Bethel Park.<P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||03:16 PM||||definately let us know what you find when they look inside.<P> I had to rebuild the engine on my first car the first summer I had it. Had to beg borrow and, er beg somemore, took me a month to get it all put back together<P> and then when I ran it, it made a low, deep thumping noise. <P> I took it to a local VW bug garage - some hippy guys that only fixed VWs, he listened to it for a minute, and said " your main bearing spun, you need a new block - theres one on that car over there - if you pull the engine and tear it all down, and leave the rest of the parts, you can have the block for $50."<P>   so I had to borrow another $50, tear his engine all apart for the block, clean it up, tear my engine all apart again, and put it all back together again.<P>  after that it purred like a finely oiled sewing machine (which is good for an aircooled vw).<P>  at the time I thought that car was a curse. now I wouldnt trade that summer for anything.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Bigfieroman||06-19-2001||04:14 PM||||Well I just got the news: They did a machanical oil pressure test, they found out that it started at 25 lbs, and then dropped off to nothing as it warmed.  The lifters are out of oil, it has a really bad rap, etc.  I need a new engine.  I am going to that Fiero club meeting tonight, and hopefully these guys can help me out.  Thanks to everyone for their concern.<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Toddster||06-19-2001||04:24 PM||||BFM, I will throw my vote at the oil pump. To go from 40 to 0 instantly means that the pump has shut down. I've seen occassions where the oil drive shaft becomes rounded (its a 6 sided shaft). At that point it just spins freely without turning the gears.<P>I really think the left turn was just coincidental timing.<P>Lots of luck. keep us posted if anything interesting is discovered. <P><BR>OK, Your last post just turned up so scratch everything I just said. I vote for a new engine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Toddster (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
